oordination, numbness or weakness in the face, arm, or leg (especially on one side of the body)\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nCerebrovascular Accident / Ischemic Stroke vs Idiopathic Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  change in consciousness, confusion, dizziness, loss of balance or coordination, numbness or weakness in the face, arm, or leg (especially on one side of the body)\n  Favours second: burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n\nCerebrovascular Accident / Ischemic Stroke vs Mediastinal Mass (Thymoma, Lymphoma, or Germ Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  change in consciousness, confusion, dizziness, loss of balance or coordination, numbness or weakness in the face, arm, or leg (especially on one side of the body)\n  Favours second: chest pain, chills, cough, coughing up blood (hemoptysis), drooping eyelid\n\nTraumatic Brachial Plexopathy vs Mediastinal Mass (Thymoma, Lymphoma, or Germ Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: chest pain, chills, cough, coughing up blood (hemoptysis), drooping eyelid\n\nParaneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy vs Mediastinal Mass (Thymoma, Lymphoma, or Germ Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: chest pain, chills, cough, coughing up blood (hemoptysis), drooping eyelid\n\nIdiopathic Brachial Plexopathy vs Mediastinal Mass (Thymoma, Lymphoma, or Germ Cell Tumor):\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: chest pain, chills, cough, coughing up blood (hemoptysis), drooping eyelid\n\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm' (score=0.78):]\n  Source: Leriche syndrome. > Pathophysiology\n  In AIOD, this narrowing typically occurs in the infrarenal aorta, common iliac arteries, or external iliac arteries. Initially, collateral circulation may compensate for reduced blood flow, allowing s\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm' (score=0.78):]\n  Source: Neurology_Adams\n  Inevitably, most patients are seen first by clinicians who may not be expert in all the fine points of cerebrovascular disease. Situations arise in which critical decisions must be made regarding anti\n[RAG context for 'The patient states he has experienced these symptoms for a few weeks' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: Neurology_Adams\n  Inevitably, most patients are seen first by clinicians who may not be expert in all the fine points of cerebrovascular disease. Situations arise in which critical decisions must be made regarding anti\n[RAG context for 'The patient states he has experienced these symptoms for a few weeks' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: Three cases of Spontaneous Vertebral Artery Dissection (SVAD), resulting in two cases of Wallenberg syndrome and one case of Foville syndrome in young, healthy men. > Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes\n  Emergency physicians are often the first to diagnose and provide initial treatment to patients presenting with stroke symptoms in the hospital setting. Neurologists diagnose stroke and provide definit\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  progressive right arm weakness over weeks with recent worsening → paraneoplastic neurological syndrome → Mediastinal Mass (Thymoma, Lymp